    {
      "id": 9,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "\ud83d\udd2c Go runtime vs native \u2014 leak-class splitter (requires /metrics scrape)",
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 18,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 19,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 3
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**These panels are the diagnosis.** They populate once the `silero-vad` pod `/metrics` endpoint is scraped (add a `ServiceMonitor`/`PodMonitor` or `prometheus.io/scrape` annotations \u2014 it is **not** scraped today). `vg-silero-vad` calls ONNX Runtime through CGo, so a large share of its memory is **native C++ allocations the Go heap profiler cannot see**. Read the split: **Go heap (`heap_inuse`) climbing \u2192 Go-side leak** (pprof heap). **Go heap flat but RSS climbing \u2192 native ONNX/CGo leak** (pprof is blind; use the *Estimated Native* panel + OS tooling). **`goroutines` climbing \u2192 goroutine leak** (suspect the inference-timeout path in `ProcessChunkWithDeadline`)."
      }
    },
